            <ArticleTitle>A spontaneous hematoma arising within an intrapancreatic accessory spleen: A case report and literature review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e8092</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/MD.0000000000008092</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="RATIONAL" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Hematoma arising within an intrapancreatic accessory spleen (IPAS) is an extremely rare pathological entity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT CONCERN" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">We present the case of a 39-year-old man with acute abdominal pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DIAGNOSES" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">The patient was initially diagnosed as pancreatic cystic neoplasm according to CT and MRI imaging.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Distal pancreatectomy was conducted because of the possibility of malignancy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOMES" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Surgical resection showed that the lesion was a hematoma in an IPAS.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LESSONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our case indicated that the differential diagnosis of hematoma in IPAS should be born in mind for cases with cystic neoplasm in tail of pancreas and an epidermoid cyst arising within an intrapancreatic accessory spleen (ECIAS).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
